      "title": "dispose has no lifecycle fence for an already-queued polling callback",
      "file": "src/workflow-stop.ts",
      "locations": [
        {
          "file": "src/workflow-stop.ts",
          "line": 68,
          "lineEnd": 85,
          "symbol": "createWorkflowStopSignal"
        }
      ],
      "evidence": "dispose only clears the interval; the polling callback and check function have no disposed state check before invoking readWorkflowStopIntent.",
      "evidenceQuotes": [
        "const check = async (): Promise<void> => {",
        "const timer = setInterval(() => void check(), pollMs);",
        "dispose: () => clearInterval(timer),"
      ],
      "reviewerEvidenceQuotes": [
        "const check = async (): Promise<void> => {",
        "const timer = setInterval(() => void check(), pollMs);",
        "dispose: () => clearInterval(timer),"
      ],
      "rationale": "If an interval callback has already been queued when dispose runs, clearing the timer alone does not fence that callback from entering check and starting a new disk read, conflicting with S5. The async read also remains active after disposal.",
      "recommendedAction": "Track a disposed boolean, set it before clearing the timer, and check it at the start of check (and before any subsequent polling work).",
